````{exercise} Diagonalization of a Hermitian operator
:label: pb:operatordiagonalization
:class: dropdown
Suppose we have a Hermitian operator&nbsp;$\hat{Q}$ with a complete, orthonormal set of eigenfunctions&nbsp;$f_n(x)$ and corresponding eigenvalues&nbsp;$q_n$.
1. Show that for any function $f(x)$ we can now write
	```{math}
	\hat{Q} f(x) = \sum_{n=1}^\infty c_n q_n f_n(x),
	```
	where, as before, the $c_n$ are the coefficients of the decomposition of $f(x)$ in the basis $f_n(x)$ (equation&nbsp;{eq}`eigenfunctionexpansioncoefficient`).
1. Show that for each of the eigenfunctions we have
	```{math}
	\Ket{f_n(x)} = \left[\sum_{k=1}^\infty \Ket{f_k(x)} \Bra{f_k(x)}\right] \Ket{f_n(x)},
	```
	and identify the operator inside the square brackets.
1. Evaluate $\sum_n c_n \hat{Q} \Ket{f_n(x)}$, and from your answer, read off that you can write&nbsp;$\hat{Q}$ as given in equation {eq}`operatordiagonalization`.
````

````{exercise} Commutator identities
:label: pb:commutatoridentities
:class: dropdown
Prove the following three commutator identities:
```{math}
:label: commutatoridentities
\begin{align*}
\left[\hat{A}+\hat{B}, \hat{C}\right] &= \left[\hat{A}, \hat{C}\right] + \left[\hat{B}, \hat{C}\right] \\
\left[\hat{A}\hat{B}, \hat{C}\right] &= \hat{A} \left[\hat{B}, \hat{C}\right] + \left[\hat{A}, \hat{C}\right] \hat{B} \\
\left[\hat{A}, \left[\hat{B}, \hat{C}\right]\right] + \left[\hat{B}, \left[\hat{C}, \hat{A}\right]\right] + \left[\hat{C}, \left[\hat{A}, \hat{B}\right]\right] &= 0.
\end{align*}
```
````

```{exercise} Eigenfunctions of two non-commuting operators
:label: pb:noncommutingoperatorseigenfunctions
:class: dropdown
Prove that two non-commuting Hermitian operators cannot share a complete set of eigenfunctions. To do so, show the inverse: if the Hermitian operators $\hat{A}$ and $\hat{B}$ do share a complete set of eigenfunctions, then for any function $f(x)$ in our Hilbert space, we get $\left[\hat{A}, \hat{B}\right] f(x) = 0$.
```

````{exercise} Ehrenfest theorem
:label: pb:Ehrenfesttheorem
:class: dropdown

```{index} Ehrenfest theorem
```
The *Ehrenfest theorem* tells us that the expectation values of quantum mechanical quantities obey the corresponding classical laws of physics. In particular, if a force is conservative, the following classical law holds
```{math}
\frac{d\mathbf{p}}{dt} = -\nabla V.
```
Show that the same relation holds for the expectation value of the respective quantum operators (in one dimension), i.e. show that
```{math}
:label: EhrenfestThmForce
\frac{\mathrm{d}\Braket{\hat{p}}}{\mathrm{d}t} = \Braket{-\frac{\partial \hat{V}}{\partial x}}.
```
NB: You could of course prove equation&nbsp;{eq}`EhrenfestThmForce` by invoking equation&nbsp;{eq}`operatorexpectationvaluetimeevolution`, the challenge here is to do it without invoking the generalized theorem.
````

````{exercise} Hellman-Feynman theorem
:label: pb:HFtheorem
:class: dropdown

```{index} Hellman-Feynman theorem
```
Prove the *Hellman-Feynman theorem*: If the Hamiltonian is dependent on a parameter $u$, then its eigenvalues (energies) also be functions of $u$, and satisfy:
```{math}
:label: HellmanFeynmantheorem
\frac{\mathrm{d}E}{\mathrm{d}u} = \Braket{\frac{\partial \hat{H}}{\partial u}}.
```
````

````{exercise} Spatial translations and quantum mechanical operators
:label: pb:translatedpositionmomentumoperators
:class: dropdown
In this problem we'll study the effect of a translation in space on the position, momentum, and composite operators.
1. Show that a translation over a distance&nbsp;$a$ leads to a translated version of the position  operator given by $\hat{x}' = \hat{x} + a$.
1. Show that a translation leaves the momentum operator unchanged: $\hat{p}' = \hat{p}$.
1. We consider an arbitrary operator $\hat{Q}$ that can be written as a power series combination of position and momentum operators:
	```{math}
	\hat{Q}(\hat{x}, \hat{p}) = \sum_{k=0}^\infty \sum_{n=0}^\infty a_{kn} \hat{x}^k \hat{p}^n.
	```
	Show that a translation over $a$ gives a translated operator $\hat{Q}' = \hat{Q}(\hat{x} + a, \hat{p})$.
````

[^1]: Since electromagnetic waves all travel at the speed of light, $c$, which as we know from special relativity is a universal constant, we can interchange the frequency $\nu$ and wavelength $\lambda$ of the radiation freely through $\nu\lambda = c$.

[^2]: Adapted from image by [Darth Kule](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/User:Darth_Kule), obtained from [Wikimedia commons](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_body.svg), public domain.

[^3]: Einstein did so in a paper he wrote in 1905, his 'annus mirabilis', or miracle year. In the same year, he also published a paper explaining Brownian motion and deriving the Stokes-Einstein relation, a third paper in which he presented the theory of special relativity and a fourth paper with his famous $E=mc^2$.

[^4]: The chance of two photons hitting the same electron simultaneously is so small that it can safely be ignored.

[^5]: To the early nineteenth century - so a full 100 years before Planck and Einstein came up with the energy quantization idea.

[^6]: If you've studied special relativity, you know that $E=mc^2$ is a special case (particle at rest) of the more general equation $E^2 = m^2 c^4 + p^2 c^2$, which combines the rest and kinetic energy of a particle with mass&nbsp;$m$ and momentum&nbsp;$p$. Photons have no mass, so for them this equation simplifies to $E = p c$, showing that if a photon has nonzero energy, it also must have nonzero momentum. Because photons have momentum, they can exert forces if they collide with a surface; applications include optical tweezers and solar sails.

[^7]: Image by [Jan Homann](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/User:Jan_Homann), obtained from [Wikimedia commons](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Visible_spectrum_of_hydrogen.jpg), CC BY-SA 3.0.

[^8]: As we'll see later, whether a specific property actually is quantized depends on the properties of the system at large.

[^9]: Measurement errors of course still exist, but even a perfect measuring device wouldn't save you here.

[^10]: As this section is rather abstract, it may help to immediately apply the ideas to a concrete example. Therefore, you could skip ahead to {numref}`sec:inifinitesquarewell` for the simplest case, and return here with an example in hand.

[^11]: To the understandable chagrin of their mathematician friends.

[^12]: In case the eigenvalue is degenerate, i.e., there are multiple eigenstates with the same eigenvalue, after the measurement the system will be in a linear combination of the eigenstates corresponding to the measured eigenvalue.

[^13]: Of course, over time the position may change again (or at least its probability distribution, which, by the second axiom, evolves according to the Schr&ouml;dinger equation).

[^14]: Examples are the Hamiltonian of the infinite square well ({numref}`sec:inifinitesquarewell`) and that of the harmonic potential ({numref}`sec:harmonicpotential`).

[^15]: The calculations here are closely related to those of the Hamiltonian of the free particle in {numref}`sec:freeparticles`, which is hardly surprising as that Hamiltonian has only a kinetic energy part, which essentially is the square of the momentum operator.

[^16]: Note that the operator we apply first is the rightmost one, just like when multiplying a vector with multiple matrices you read from right to left.

[^17]: Cartoon by Randall Munroe, from [xkcd.com/1473/](https://xkcd.com/1473/), CC BY-SA-NC 2.5.

[^18]: We will be using the results of {numref}`sec:TISE` in these proofs. I have chosen to put this section here to keep all the fundamental results together, while {numref}`sec:TISE` fits well in {numref}`Chapter {number} <ch:SEsolutions>`.

[^19]: This concept generalizes to any function&nbsp;$\Psi(x)$, also if it has no Taylor series expansion. In that case, we define the translation operator&nbsp;$\hat{T}(a)$ through its spectral decomposition (cf. equation&nbsp;{eq}`operatordiagonalization`) in the momentum basis:
	```{math}
	\begin{align*}
	\hat{T}(a) &= \int_{-\infty}^\infty \exp\left(-\frac{ia}{\hbar} p\right) \ket{p} \bra{p} \,\mathrm{d}p, \\
	\hat{T}(a) \Psi(x) &= \int_{-\infty}^\infty \exp\left(-\frac{ia}{\hbar} p\right) \,f_p(x) \,\Phi(p) \,\mathrm{d}p,
	\end{align*}
	```
	where the $f_p(x)$ are the eigenfunctions of the momentum operator (eq.&nbsp;{eq}`momentumeigenfunctions`) and $\Phi(p)$ is the momentum space representation (i.e. the Fourier transform) of $\Psi(x)$ (cf. equation&nbsp;{eq}`MomentumFouriercomponents`):
	```{math}
	\Phi(p) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2 \pi \hbar}} \int_{-\infty}^\infty e^{-i p x / \hbar} \,\Psi(x) \,\mathrm{d}x.
	```

```{index} translationally invariant system
```
[^20]: Also known as *translationally invariant*.

[^21]: See [Section 4.2](https://interactivetextbooks.tudelft.nl/nb1140/content/momentum.html#conservation-of-momentum) of *[Introduction to particle and continuum mechanics](https://interactivetextbooks.tudelft.nl/nb1140/index.html)*.

[^22]: We're again assuming that the Taylor series exists. For the last equality, we also need that the Hamiltonian itself is independent of time. The corresponding case for a Hamiltonian that is time-dependent is significantly more work.

